## The IssueTrackerAdapter API

Every adapter exposes the same capability groups regardless of backend:

| Group       | Operations                                                                                            |
| ----------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `issues`    | `ready_queue`, `search_issues`, `get_issue`, `create_issue`, `update_issue`, `close_issue`            |
| `comments`  | `list_comments`, `add_comment`                                                                        |
| `worktrees` | `worktree_create`, `worktree_list`, `worktree_remove`, `worktree_tool_name()`, `worktrees_base_dir()` |
| `sync`      | `sync_push()`, `sync_pull()`                                                                          |
| `labels`    | `label_catalog`, `add_label`, `remove_label`, `map_label_to_native`, `map_native_to_label`            |
| `blockers`  | `add_blocker`, `list_blockers`, `remove_blocker`                                                      |

`ready_queue` returns only **unblocked, open** issues (the next-work queue) —
not every issue. To browse the full board, use `holly tracker list`, the
adapter-neutral CLI surface for `search_issues`; it accepts `--status`,
`--type`, `--assignee`, `--label` (repeatable), `--query`, and `--limit`
filters, and `--json` for machine-readable output (the default in non-TTY/agent
contexts). `holly tracker label list` is the read-only discovery surface for
canonical orchestration labels and adapter-native labels. Holly labels describe
what an issue means to orchestration (`holly:type:*`, `holly:track:*`,
`holly:wave:*`, `holly:blocked-by:*`); ticket lifecycle status remains the
tracker status field.

Write labels with `holly tracker label add`/`holly tracker label remove`:

```bash
holly tracker label add <id> [<id> ...] --label <name> [--label <name> ...]
holly tracker label remove <id> [<id> ...] --label <name>
```

The label is always a **named** flag (`--label`), never a trailing positional,
so the adapter's label-ordering footgun is unrepresentable. `add` validates
each label against the catalog and **rejects** unknown labels and issue-ID-shaped
values (with a nearest-match hint), so a typo can't silently create a junk
label. `remove` is permissive — it can delete any label, including a bogus one
created before this guard existed. Both echo the resulting label set per issue
(`--json` for machine-readable output). They map onto the adapter `add_label`/
`remove_label` methods, so behaviour is identical across adapters.

For the orchestration `wave` and `track` families, prefer the dedicated
setters over raw `label add` — you supply only the value and Holly owns the
`holly:` namespace:

```bash
holly tracker wave  set <id> [<id> ...] --wave 2      # writes holly:wave:2
holly tracker track set <id> [<id> ...] --track infra # writes holly:track:infra
```

Both are **single-valued**: setting a new wave/track replaces any existing
label in that family, so an issue can't end up in two waves. `--wave` must be a
positive integer; `--track` must be a single token (no `:` or whitespace).

To compute waves for a whole epic from its dependency graph instead of by hand,
use `holly tracker wave plan <epic>`:

```bash
holly tracker wave plan <epic>           # dry-run: print the layering
holly tracker wave plan <epic> --apply   # write the holly:wave:N labels
```

It layers the epic's children by their in-epic dependencies
(`wave = 1 + max(wave of deps)`). Dry-run is the safe default; `--apply`
commits via the single-valued wave setter. A dependency cycle is reported and
refused. (Requires a tracker that models parent-child hierarchy; trackers
without one report no children.)

`holly tracker create` accepts first-class orchestration flags so a wired-up
child needs only one command instead of create → dep add → label add:

```bash
holly tracker create "Child task" --parent <epic> \
    --depends-on <id> --depends-on <id> --track backend --wave 1
```

`--parent` is applied natively at create time (preserving the tracker's
hierarchical child numbering); `--depends-on` (repeatable), `--track`, and
`--wave` are applied immediately after creation via the same blocker/label
surface. Parent and dependency IDs are validated up front, so a typo fails
before any half-wired issue is created.

`holly tracker show <id>` surfaces the orchestration view — Type, Track, Wave,
and the full label set — alongside the issue detail (and `--json` includes
`labels`), so you can read an issue's wave/track placement at a glance.

Wire dependencies between existing issues with `holly tracker dep`:

```bash
holly tracker dep add    <id> --on <id> [--on <id> ...]   # <id> is blocked by each --on
holly tracker dep remove <id> --on <id>
holly tracker dep list   <id>                              # what blocks <id>
```

The blocker is a **named** flag (`--on`), never a positional, so the
blocked/blocker direction can't be swapped by mistake. `add` validates every
ID, rejects self-dependencies, and refuses edges that would create a cycle.

The `null` adapter implements everything as a no-op — useful for
projects that do not want issue tracking at all.

